"var Component=(()=>{var g=Object.create;var a=Object.defineProperty;var m=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var p=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var u=Object.getPrototypeOf,b=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var f=(t,e)=>()=>(e||t((e={exports:{}}).exports,e),e.exports),w=(t,e)=>{for(var i in e)a(t,i,{get:e[i],enumerable:!0})},s=(t,e,i,o)=>{if(e&&typeof e==\"object\"||typeof e==\"function\")for(let r of p(e))!b.call(t,r)&&r!==i&&a(t,r,{get:()=>e[r],enumerable:!(o=m(e,r))||o.enumerable});return t};var y=(t,e,i)=>(i=t!=null?g(u(t)):{},s(e||!t||!t.__esModule?a(i,\"default\",{value:t,enumerable:!0}):i,t)),v=t=>s(a({},\"__esModule\",{value:!0}),t);var h=f((A,l)=>{l.exports=_jsx_runtime});var k={};w(k,{default:()=>d});var n=y(h());function c(t){let e={a:\"a\",code:\"code\",em:\"em\",h2:\"h2\",img:\"img\",li:\"li\",ol:\"ol\",p:\"p\",pre:\"pre\",strong:\"strong\",ul:\"ul\",...t.components};return(0,n.jsxs)(n.Fragment,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:'We keep asking the wrong question about models. \"Which one is smartest?\" is a benchmark-leaderboard question, and it has a stable answer: whatever frontier lab shipped most recently.'}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"The question that actually decides whether your agent ships is different: \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"which model behaves best inside a loop?\"}),' Not \"how much does it know\" but \"can it run for forty tool-calling turns, keep the plan coherent, recover from a failed test run, and stop when the task is done.\"']}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:\"Sebastian Raschka put this distinction on a slide better than I can:\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:(0,n.jsx)(e.img,{src:\"/blog/intelligence-vs-behavior.svg\",alt:\"Intelligence vs Behavior - idea by Sebastian Raschka\"})}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"On the intelligence axis: GPT-4.5-class and Gemini 3.1 Pro-class models. On the behavior axis: GLM-5.3-Flash. Twice. Same model competing with frontier intelligence on \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"agentic execution\"}),\" - at $0.15 per million input tokens instead of $5-15.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"That is not a model-quality miracle. It is an \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"inference engineering\"}),\" story, and it is the story I want to tell here - because inference engineering is quietly becoming the discipline that separates teams who ship agents from teams who demo them.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.h2,{children:\"The Two Leaderboards That Matter\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:\"Look at Z.ai's release benchmarks for GLM-5.3-Flash against the heavyweights, and read them as two different leaderboards:\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:(0,n.jsx)(e.img,{src:\"/blog/benchmark-chart.svg\",alt:\"GLM-5.3-Flash release benchmarks across six benchmarks\"})}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Where it loses - raw intelligence:\"}),\" HLE w/ Tools (55.3 vs GPT-5.6 Terra's 57.9), Agents' Last Exam (26.3 vs Gemini 3.7 Flash's 28.0), GDPval-AA behind GLM-5.2's own deeper-thinking numbers in absolute knowledge-density terms. Nobody should claim a 320B-A18B model out-reasons a frontier giant on pure reasoning. It does not, and that is fine.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Where it wins - agentic behavior:\"}),\" DeepSWE v1.1 at \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"63.4 vs Claude Opus 4.8's 58.0\"}),\". AutomationBench v1.0.6 at \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"48.8 vs Opus 4.8's 37.2\"}),\" - a 31% relative gap over a model that costs roughly 34x more per token. Terminal Bench 2.1 at 84.3, within two points of the best frontier models.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"Hold those two facts together: \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"loses on knowledge, wins on execution\"}),\". For a chat assistant that would be a weird trade. For an agent that edits code, runs tests, and operates tools all day, it is exactly the trade you want - because in a loop, execution quality compounds and raw knowledge mostly just sits in context.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.h2,{children:\"Why Agentic Workloads Are an Inference Problem\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"A chat message is mostly \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"decode\"}),\" - emitting a few hundred tokens. Economically trivial. An agentic loop is mostly \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"prefill\"}),\" - re-processing hundreds of thousands of context tokens on every tool-call turn, over and over, for 30-50 turns.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:\"That flips which parts of the architecture matter:\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.pre,{children:(0,n.jsx)(e.code,{className:\"language-mermaid\",children:`graph LR\\r\n    A[\"Chat workload<br/>small context, mostly decode\"] --> C[\"Cost is fine on anything\"]\\r\n    B[\"Agentic workload<br/>re-prefill 100Ks of tokens<br/>every tool-call turn\"] --> D[\"Cost = attention + KV cache\"]\\r\n    D --> E[\"Cheap attention -> longer loops\"]\\r\n    D --> F[\"Small KV cache -> more parallel agents\"]\\r\n    E --> G[\"Agentic behavior<br/>becomes affordable\"]\\r\n    F --> G\n`})}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"Raw intelligence scales with parameters and training compute - the expensive axis. Agentic behavior scales with \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"how many loop-iterations you can afford per dollar\"}),\", which is an inference-engineering lever. That is the thesis: the labs that engineer inference best can buy behavior without buying intelligence.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.h2,{children:\"How You Engineer Behavior Instead of Scaling Intelligence\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:\"Sebastian Raschka's architecture notes on GLM-5.3-Flash are the best public teardown of this, and the details matter more than the marketing:\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.ul,{children:[`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"A 3:1 hybrid attention stack\"}),\" - 34 Kimi Delta Attention (KDA) layers interleaved with 11 full-attention MLA / DeepSeek Sparse Attention layers. Here is the clever part: Kimi uses KDA \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"plus\"}),\" full attention, and DeepSeek V3.2 uses DSA \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"plus\"}),\" full attention. GLM-5.3-Flash combines \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"two efficient mechanisms\"}),' - a \"super hybrid\" where even its full-attention layers are compressed. That is where the reported 3x attention-compute and 4.4x KV-cache reductions come from.']}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"A aggressively shrunk MoE\"}),\" - from GLM-5.2's 744B total / 40B active parameters down to \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"320B total / 18B active\"}),\". Roughly 2.3x fewer total parameters and 2.2x fewer active per token. This is the intelligence budget being deliberately spent down.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"A DeepSeek V4-style mHC residual path\"}),\" with four parallel streams - training-stability engineering borrowed across labs, because stable hyperparameters let you retrain smaller models faster.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[\"A native vision encoder, so the agent can \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"see\"}),\" rendered UIs and screenshots in its coding loop rather than only read text.\"]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"Now stack that against the workload math from the last section. The parameters you delete mostly hurt the knowledge/reasoning axis (HLE, GDPval) - the intelligence benchmarks where Flash concedes ground. The attention engineering mostly helps the loop axis: long contexts, many turns, high concurrency - the behavior benchmarks where it wins. \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"The architecture is shaped like the thesis.\"})]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.pre,{children:(0,n.jsx)(e.code,{className:\"language-mermaid\",children:`graph TB\\r\n    S[\"Shrink MoE<br/>744B-A40B -> 320B-A18B\"] --> L[\"Loses a little<br/>raw intelligence\"]\\r\n    S --> W[\"Gains a lot of<br/>behavior-per-dollar\"]\\r\n    H[\"34 KDA + 11 MLA/DSA layers<br/>3:1 super hybrid\"] --> W\\r\n    Q[\"Cache quantization +<br/>EPD disaggregated serving\"] --> W\n`})}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.h2,{children:\"What the Industry Should Copy (Regardless of Vendor)\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:\"I want to be careful not to turn this into a Z.ai ad, so let us extract the transferable engineering:\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.ol,{children:[`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Disaggregate prefill from decode.\"}),\" Z.ai's Encode-Prefill-Decode (EPD) architecture runs encoding, prefill, and decoding as independently scalable worker pools. Agentic traffic has wildly swinging prefill-to-decode ratios turn to turn; monolithic serving leaves hardware idle. Every serious serving stack (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM) is converging on this.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Quantize the cache, not just the weights.\"}),\" Hybrid INT8/FP8/BF16 KV-cache quantization cut Flash's cache footprint 4.44x vs GLM-5.3. Cache memory is the binding constraint on how many concurrent agent loops you can serve.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Let models help build their own serving layer.\"}),\" Z.ai used a GLM-5.3-powered agent to write kernels and find bottlenecks, reporting a 3x serving improvement - including serving on domestic Chinese chips at NVIDIA-parity per-token cost. Whatever hardware you have, an agent-in-the-loop optimization cycle is now a proven technique.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Price follows engineering, not just scale.\"}),\" Flash launched at a limited-time 50% discount ($0.15/$0.50 per MTok; list is around double that). Even at list price it is ~10x under Opus 4.6 - because the architecture, not subsidies, moved the cost curve.\"]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.h2,{children:\"The Honest Trade-offs\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:\"To keep this credible: Flash is not uniformly better. It gives up ground on pure reasoning and knowledge benchmarks (HLE, Agents Last Exam). Its pricing is promotional and will rise. It is a young model with less ecosystem hardening than Claude in agentic tooling. And release-time vendor benchmarks deserve the same skepticism you would apply to any vendor - which is exactly why the next section matters.\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"A cheap model is only a win if it is \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"good enough for your task\"}),\", and the only way to know is measurement. Benchmark scores are the vendor claim; your eval set is your claim. This is the core of my post on \",(0,n.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"/blog/llm-evals\",children:\"LLM evaluation\"}),\", applied here with one twist: \",(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"evaluate behavior, not intelligence.\"}),\" Task completion rate over 40 turns, tool-call accuracy, cost per \",(0,n.jsx)(e.em,{children:\"completed\"}),\" task, tokens per task - not MMLU-style scores. A model that finishes in 30% fewer tokens is 30% cheaper on top of the sticker discount.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.h2,{children:\"The Playbook\"}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.ol,{children:[`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Route, do not marry.\"}),\" Default the bulk of agent steps to the cost-efficient tier; escalate the hardest 5% of reasoning to a frontier model. The bill barely notices the escalation.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Evaluate on loop metrics\"}),\" - completion rate, tool accuracy, tokens-per-task - before swapping any model in production.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Design for cache hits\"}),\" - stable system prompts and prefix-stable context are priced 5-10x cheaper everywhere.\"]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsxs)(e.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(e.strong,{children:\"Treat serving as a product surface\"}),\" - disaggregated prefill/decode and cache quantization are where the cost curves are actually moving.\"]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,(0,n.jsx)(e.p,{children:\"The intelligence leaderboard will keep being won by whoever trains next. But the behavior leaderboard - the one that decides what agents you can afford to run - is being won by inference engineering. GLM-5.3-Flash matching frontier models twice on that axis at $0.15 per million tokens is the clearest evidence yet that the next big thing in AI is not a bigger model. It is a better loop.\"})]})}function d(t={}){let{wrapper:e}=t.components||{};return e?(0,n.jsx)(e,{...t,children:(0,n.jsx)(c,{...t})}):c(t)}return v(k);})();\n;return Component;"